# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build context exclusions.
# Anything listed here never reaches the Docker daemon, so it can never be
# baked into a layer — not even an intermediate one that is later deleted.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Secrets. The image must be built with zero credentials inside it; keys are
# supplied at run time via env_file / -e.
# No exception is made for .env.example. It is a template that developers edit
# in place, so treating it as safe-by-name is exactly how real keys get shipped.
# Nothing in the image needs it.
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
secrets/
.azure/
.aws/
.ssh/

# Version control and CI metadata (~90 MB of history, useless at run time).
.git
.github
.gitignore
.gitattributes

# Generated vector stores. These are rebuilt on first use and live on a
# Docker volume, not in the image.
chromadb/
.chromadb/
vector_store/

# Python build and cache artifacts from the host. Host-built bytecode targets
# a different interpreter path and would shadow the image's own.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Host virtualenvs. Linux-incompatible binaries and the single largest
# accidental-bloat risk in the context.
venv/
.venv/
env/

# Build and run logs. Harmless in size but they can contain stack traces,
# hostnames and registry URLs, and they change on every build, which needlessly
# invalidates the COPY layer cache.
*.log

# Editor, OS and tooling noise.
.vscode/
.idea/
.cursor/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# Docs and container recipes — not needed by the running process.
docs/
*.md
!README.md
Dockerfile*
docker-compose*.yml
.dockerignore
LICENSE
